{"node":{"id":"HP:0032988","label":"Persistent head lag","ic":5.799,"definition":"The Premie-Neuro and the Dubowitz Neurological Examination score head lag in the same manner. Scoring for both is as follows: 0 = head drops and stays back, 1 = tries to lift head but drops it back, 2 = able to lift head slightly, 3 = lifts head in line with body, and 4 = head in front of body. This term applies if head lag persists beyond an expected age at a level of 0 or 1. Persistent head lag beyond age 4 mo has been linked to poor outcomes."},"diseases":[{"id":"MONDO:0007298","label":"spinocerebellar ataxia type 29","in_core":false,"edge":{"id":"e_45ad23e771c2","source":"MONDO:0007298","target":"HP:0032988","type":"has_phenotype","evidence":[{"db":"HPO","origin":"OMIM","record":"OMIM:117360 · PMID:37964426","url":"https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/37964426/","level":"observed","date":"2024-07-17","confidence":1.0,"snippet":"Spinocerebellar ataxia 29, congenital nonprogressive: Persistent head lag (frequency 1/1) [PCS]"}],"contradicting":[],"confidence":1.0,"independent_sources":["OMIM"],"level":"observed"},"confidence":1.0},{"id":"MONDO:0009504","label":"mitochondrial DNA depletion syndrome 9","in_core":false,"edge":{"id":"e_93aba28261ec","source":"MONDO:0009504","target":"HP:0032988","type":"has_phenotype","evidence":[{"db":"HPO","origin":"OMIM","record":"OMIM:245400 · PMID:19526370","url":"https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/19526370/","level":"observed","date":"2020-10-11","confidence":1.0,"snippet":"Mitochondrial DNA depletion syndrome 9 (encephalomyopathic type with methylmalonic aciduria): Persistent head lag (frequency 1/1) [PCS]"}],"contradicting":[],"confidence":1.0,"independent_sources":["OMIM"],"level":"observed"},"confidence":1.0},{"id":"MONDO:0010235","label":"X-linked intellectual disability-psychosis-macroorchidism syndrome","in_core":false,"edge":{"id":"e_0d0366de2f3a","source":"MONDO:0010235","target":"HP:0032988","type":"has_phenotype","evidence":[{"db":"HPO","origin":